Badges

Corrected after user review (November 2026). The first ECORP build drew the rail badge inside the 36x32 icon box (top 1px, right 2px, 10px text), so it sat on top of the bell. The user rejected it as not Slack's design system.

Rail (Activity, DMs): red pill with a number, 20px tall, 11px bold white, 2px 
#38123c ring. Anchor it to the icon box at top -6px, right -8px so it hangs off the top-right corner and the glyph stays readable. Put the badge element inside the icon box (<i>…<b>1</b></i>), not on the label.
Rail with unreads but no mentions: use b.dot, the dimmed 
#7a607d dot (see Rail and top bar).
Sidebar rows (unread mentions on a channel or DM): .badge pill aligned right, same red, and the row text goes bold white (a.b).
Unread without mentions: bold white row text only, no badge.
Check badges at device scale factor 3 against a real Slack screenshot before delivering. The red (
#cd2553) was picked to match Slack's mention badge but has not been checked against a screenshot; a screenshot from the user overrides it.
Initials avatars

When no photo is supplied, use a coloured tile with white initials. In the 20px sidebar DM tile, initials need 7.5px text with -0.2px letter spacing, or two-letter initials like "MJ" get clipped. Message-row tiles (36px) take 14px weight 900. For a bot or app whose logo is a dark mark (for example the Auth0 shield), put the logo on a white tile with a 1px #ddd border.

Thread panel

Built in the ECORP / Auth0 demo (November 2026), not yet checked against a real Slack screenshot. Panel 50% width, min 600px. Header 52px: "Thread" in 17px weight 900, then "# channel-name" in 13px 
#616061, with kebab and close on the right. The parent message comes first, then an "N replies" label in 13px 
#616061 with a hairline running to the right. The composer shows "Reply…" with an "Also send to #channel" checkbox below it. In the channel, the parent message shows "N replies" in 
#1264a3 bold, the replier avatars at 24px, and "Last reply today at <time>" in grey. Update the counts on both sides each time a reply posts.

Agent response pattern

Each beat is two steps: prompt, then response.

Clicking the composer pastes the full prompt instantly (no typing animation) and turns the send button green.
Pressing send posts the user's message (avatar, name, "Today at <time>").
A typing line shows "<Bot> is running <action name>" with three dots for about 1.6 seconds.
The bot message appears, in this order:
Tool line in grey: "Used <action> from <server> (<vendor>) MCP ›"
A short narrative paragraph that leads with the one thing needing attention
The widget
A closing question offering the next action
Reaction icons at the bottom right

Button-driven agents (alert card, then "Investigate", then "View Recommendations", then "Apply") follow the user's prototype instead: the button triggers the reply directly, with no composer step. Progress steps are bordered rows (1px #ddd, radius 8px) with a spinner that turns into a black check circle, about 0.9 seconds each, and each row has a chevron that expands a one-line detail.

Widget card inside a message
Card: 1px 
#cfcfcf border, radius 14px, padding 18px 20px, max-width 820px, 10px top margin.
Header: icon, title, sub-line naming the source and time, a status pill on the right.
Progress bar with labels beneath (count on the left, percentage on the right).
Tile grid: 3 columns, 8px gap, max-width 560px. Tiles have a 1px 
#cfcfcf border, radius 12px, padding 14px 8px 12px, with an icon, a name and a Clear / Blocked state.
Alert row for the blocked item, with a note line.
Actions: one black primary button and one secondary button.
Widget content follows the real payload envelope used for the demo; do not invent schema shapes.
Verify before delivering
Render with Playwright at 1600x1000 and device scale factor 2. Then screenshot individual elements up close: rail icons and badges at scale factor 4; top bar, sidebar DM tiles, panel header and a message row at scale factor 3.
Put a rendered crop next to the user's reference screenshot at the same scale (a 2x reference against a 2x render) and compare them by eye. Sample the reference's pixel colours instead of guessing hex values.
Parse the inline script with Node to catch syntax errors before publishing.
Compare against the user's reference screenshot. Where they differ, the screenshot wins.
Flag data inconsistencies in the user's prototype (for example a "last checked" time earlier than the start time) instead of silently copying or fixing them.
Deliver one self-contained HTML file with assets embedded.
Not covered

Dark theme, mobile Slack, huddles and canvases were not built in the source demo. Threads were built once (see Thread panel) but not verified. Ask for a reference screenshot before attempting any of them.
